msgid "Two tiers"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*One study, two faithful shapes: a property graph to work in, RDF and "
"CIDOC CRM to reason and share.*"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"EMStudio keeps the Extended Matrix in **two tiers at once**. This is a "
"deliberate choice of design, and each tier does what the other does "
"badly."
msgstr ""

msgid "The property-graph tier"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"While you work, the study is a **property graph**: nodes and relations, "
"each with its fields. This is the shape that makes the editor responsive:"
" laying out a large matrix, folding groups, connecting units with a drag "
"of the handle, filtering, undoing. The ``em.json`` file is this tier "
"written to disk (see :doc:`/reference/emjson`). A property graph is the "
"right shape for *doing* the work."
msgstr ""

msgid "The RDF and CIDOC CRM tier"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The same study projects to **RDF**, mapped onto **CIDOC CRM** and its "
"archaeological extensions. This is the right shape for *reasoning about* "
"the record and *sharing* it: queries in SPARQL, exchange with other "
"semantic systems, a formal and standard vocabulary. ``File ▸ Export ▸ "
"Turtle (.ttl)`` writes it (see :doc:`/reference/export-formats`)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The projection is made by the s3Dgraphy library, the same one that every "
"EM tool uses, and the library can also read it back. The round trip, from"
" the graph to RDF and back to a graph identical to the first, is how the "
"projection is shown to lose nothing."
msgstr ""

msgid "Why both"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Tools built on triples are rigorous but heavy to edit; tools built on "
"property graphs are quick but semantically loose. EMStudio keeps both: "
"you edit in the property graph and reason in RDF, and the two are the "
"same study seen in two ways. A study made here is at once a working "
"document and a knowledge graph that can be queried."
msgstr ""

msgid "Neither tier comes first"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The study is not \"really\" triples with a graph on top, nor \"really\" a"
" graph with an export to triples. It is one record with two faithful "
"projections. The narrative, the exports and the other tools all read the "
"same record, each in the tier that suits what it does."
msgstr ""

